      "id": 1,
      "text": "Union was established by 1833 and incorporated in 1838, named for its role linking Florence and Big Bone Lick, a salt‑production hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Union_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Union’s motto, ‘Proud Past, Promising Future,’ highlights its historic roots and growth in one of Kentucky’s fastest‑growing counties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Union_Kentucky",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Union may trace its origins to the late 1700s, with the Fowler family owning much of the land in the early 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Union_Kentucky",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Union’s public library, Scheben Branch, is part of the Boone County Public Library system.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Union remained rural until the 1990s and 2000s, when residential growth began reshaping the community.",
